"""model-gen — a 3D model no design package supplies: an orthographic blueprint first, then a
Blender-built model authored against it.

The 3D sibling of `Tools/pf/asset-gen.py`, whose `codex exec` door it reuses. When to reach for it,
and what to do with the result: the `pf-model-gen` skill.

    python3 Tools/pf/model-gen.py blueprint        <request.json> [--variants N] [--dry-run]
    python3 Tools/pf/model-gen.py select-blueprint <request.json> <variant>
    python3 Tools/pf/model-gen.py build            <request.json> [--variants N] [--rounds N] [--dry-run]
    python3 Tools/pf/model-gen.py select           <request.json> <variant> [--round K] [--force]
    python3 Tools/pf/model-gen.py rebuild          <request.json>
    python3 Tools/pf/model-gen.py status           [<request.json>...] [--under DIR] [--md OUT]

  blueprint         Codex's built-in image_gen draws a 2x2 orthographic sheet (FRONT, RIGHT / BACK,
                    TOP) of the request's subject at its size and in its palette, once per variant.
                    Every variant is measured: four views, none crossing its cell, each view's
                    proportions against the requested size, one scale across the views.
  select-blueprint  Promote one variant to `<out>/blueprint.png` (+ `.gen.json`, status
                    pending-user-review). A person approving it is the model's design sign-off.
  build             Codex (read-only, no workspace) writes a Blender Python script against the selected
                    blueprint. The TOOL runs it in Blender headless (`model-gen-blender.py`), checks the
                    result (bounding box, pivot, triangle budget, palette materials, named parts),
                    exports FBX, renders the blueprint's four views and scores each silhouette against
                    the blueprint; the comparison goes back to Codex for the next round. One chain of
                    rounds per variant, chains in parallel.
  select            Promote a variant's best passing round to `<out>/`: build.py (the source),
                    `<id>.fbx`, views.png, hero.png, compare.png, model.gen.json (status
                    pending-user-review). --force takes a round that failed a check.
  rebuild           Re-run Blender on the committed `<out>/build.py`, after a hand edit or a harness
                    change. The script is deterministic, so the geometry is too; FBX bytes still differ
                    by the exporter's timestamp.
  status            One row per request: blueprint and model state, and whether the committed files
                    still match their provenance. --under keeps requests whose `out` is inside DIR;
                    --md writes the table (a design package's MODELS.md).

The DATA is the SKU's own, under `Tools/ModelGen/` (`MODELGEN_ROOT`), as `Tools/AssetGen/` is to
asset-gen: `requests/<id>.json` (reviewable), the selected files wherever a request's `out` points,
and `candidates/` (scratch, git-ignored by the tool itself).

What a generation is NOT:
  * deterministic. Neither the blueprint nor the script comes back the same twice, so the selected
    files are committed SOURCE and nothing regenerates them in CI. `rebuild` re-runs the committed
    script; it never asks a model again.
  * a finished decision. Both selections carry `status: pending-user-review` until a person
    approves them.
  * an import. Nothing is written under `Assets/`: importing the FBX (scale 1, materials remapped
    by their `M_<key>` names) is the consuming story's job.

Why the model only writes text: given write access, a model post-processes its own output without
saying so (asset-gen saw a silent `sips` resize). So Codex runs `-s read-only` from a temporary
directory outside the repo, its script comes back in its reply, and the tool screens it (imports,
builtins and bpy entry points outside geometry are refused) before Blender ever runs it.

Exit codes (CliConventions): 0 ok · 1 drift (no variant passed its checks; a committed file no longer
matches its provenance) · 2 error.

Request (`schema: model-gen-request/v1`), repo-relative paths:
    id           one path segment
    subject      what the model is, in a sentence or two: the blueprint prompt and the author brief
    size         {"width", "height", "length"} metres, the OVERALL bounding box (mirrors, bumpers and
                 wheels included). Unity axes: width = X, height = Y, length = Z (the front faces +Z)
    palette      {"key": "RRGGBB", ...}: the only colours; materials come out as M_<key>
    max_tris     the triangle budget, counted after bevels
    parts        optional [{"name", "note"}]: objects that must stay separate (they move) under exactly
                 these names, origin at their pivot
    references   optional [{"path", "role"}]: approved art the look follows (style only)
    notes        optional modelling guidance for both stages
    hero         optional {"pitch", "yaw"} degrees for the 3/4 render (default 30, 35)
    out          the directory `select-blueprint` and `select` write into
    consumer     optional: the story or file that imports the FBX (recorded)

Environment: MODELGEN_ROOT (default `Tools/ModelGen`, resolved from this script's location), BLENDER_BIN
(default: Blender.app on macOS, else `blender` on PATH), MODELGEN_MODEL (default: Codex's own),
MODELGEN_EFFORT (default medium), MODELGEN_TIMEOUT_S (default 900, per Codex call),
MODELGEN_BLENDER_TIMEOUT_S (default 300), MODELGEN_IOU_TARGET (default 0.88: a chain stops early once
every check passes at this mean silhouette match). The blueprint call is an asset-gen call: ASSETGEN_*
apply. Needs network: run it unsandboxed. A build takes minutes per round: run it in the background.
"""

